WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:06.160 --> 00:00:11.760
Let’s focus on the introduction now. The first&nbsp;
sentence is one of the most important because,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:15.920
it is the first thing people read.&nbsp;
So, you have to work it and work it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:15.920 --> 00:00:21.040
until it is perfect and powerful, just&nbsp;
as the title. Then in the introduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.040 --> 00:00:27.760
make sure that you answer what we call the MRCI&nbsp;
Framework. “M” stands for motivation. What is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.760 --> 00:00:33.600
the motivation of your topic? Do you explain why&nbsp;
there is a real gap in the literature, but also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:39.600
why your research question is an interesting one,&nbsp;
a relevant one, an important one? You could also&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.600 --> 00:00:45.680
explain why your research is necessary, but why&nbsp;
no one has done it before. “R” is for results.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.640 --> 00:00:52.560
What are your findings? That's the part where&nbsp;
you answer the research question. “C” is for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.560 --> 00:00:58.880
contributions. So how do these results contribute&nbsp;
to the ongoing research or previous literature?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.840 --> 00:01:06.080
Contributions can be theoretical. They can also be&nbsp;
empirical, when you bring up new data or highlight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:06.080 --> 00:01:12.000
new phenomena. And they can be methodological, if&nbsp;
you design a new method that is better than others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:18.720
or complementary to others. And, finally, “I”&nbsp;
is for implications. What are the implications&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:18.720 --> 00:01:24.160
of your work for future research? MRCI should&nbsp;
be your guide when writing the introduction,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.160 --> 00:01:29.120
and the abstract too, since the abstract is&nbsp;
actually just a short version of the introduction.&nbsp;

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:35.280
Next , et's take a look at the literature review.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:36.240 --> 00:01:41.440
Keep in mind that most literature reviews are at&nbsp;
best very confusing, at worst downright boring.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.320 --> 00:01:47.520
So, when you write it, here are some tips to&nbsp;
make a good literature review. First of all,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.520 --> 00:01:51.440
while you need to have read everything, you&nbsp;
should not put it all down in that section.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:52.320 --> 00:01:56.880
Just write what is significant, highly relevant,&nbsp;
and helpful to get to the research problem.
 &nbsp;

00:01:57.840 --> 00:02:02.720
Always make sure that you have a purpose.&nbsp;
You're not just listing references, but you're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.720 --> 00:02:06.720
conducting an argumentation and demonstration&nbsp;
that's leading you somewhere - to the gap.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:12.400
To help with that, a good organization, a&nbsp;
proper outline of the review, are essential.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:18.160
The literature review is a delicate exercise&nbsp;
in finding the right balance between extremes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:18.160 --> 00:02:24.240
for getting references, or being too exhaustive.&nbsp;
Not being critical enough or being too critical.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:25.200 --> 00:02:27.760
Not showing enough respect&nbsp;
or showing way too much.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.600
Going back to ancient civilization times or&nbsp;
rejecting anything that's older than five years.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.320
Let's keep in mind that the literature&nbsp;
review is also a political act.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:39.280 --> 00:02:43.200
By choosing whom you are quoting, you are&nbsp;
choosing side in the research community.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.160 --> 00:02:49.840
So, treat carefully and make sure&nbsp;
you're not omitting any key reference.

